{
  "response": "# Accessibility Design Review: `BuggyAccordion`\n\n**VERDICT: REVISE**\n\n**Overall Assessment**:\nThe `BuggyAccordion` implementation correctly utilizes native `<button>` semantics, synchronizes `aria-expanded`, and hides decorative symbols with `aria-hidden=\"true\"`. However, it only implements approximately 50% of the WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices Guide (APG) Accordion pattern: triggers lack mandatory heading semantics, expanded panels lack the `region` role and `aria-labelledby` association, and conditional rendering creates dangling `aria-controls` targets when panels are collapsed.\n\n---\n\n### Pre-commitment Predictions\nBefore reviewing the code, typical accessibility gaps for custom accordion/disclosure widgets were predicted:\n1. **Missing Heading Semantics**: Accordion trigger buttons not enclosed in semantic headings (`<h[2-6]>` or `role=\"heading\"`), preventing screen reader navigation via heading shortcuts. *(Confirmed)*\n2. **Missing Region Landmark & Labeling**: Expanded content panels lacking `role=\"region\"` and `aria-labelledby` referencing the trigger button. *(Confirmed)*\n3. **Broken `aria-controls` References**: Conditional unmounting causing `aria-controls` to point to non-existent DOM nodes when collapsed. *(Confirmed)*\n4. **ID Collision Across Instances**: Un-namespaced index-based IDs (`panel-${index}`) colliding if multiple instances are rendered. *(Confirmed)*\n5. **Keyboard Navigation Shortcuts**: Missing optional APG accordion keyboard enhancements (Up/Down arrow, Home/End). *(Confirmed)*\n\n---\n\n### Critical Findings (Blocks Access)\n*No CRITICAL findings.* (The component remains keyboard operable via standard Tab/Space/Enter navigation and does not introduce a keyboard trap).\n\n---\n\n### Major Findings (Significantly Degrades Experience)\n\n#### 1. Accordion trigger button lacks heading wrapper semantics\n- **Location**: `BuggyAccordion.jsx:13-22`\n- **Impacted Users**: Screen reader users (JAWS, NVDA, VoiceOver)\n- **Standard**: WCAG 2.2 SC 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships - Level A), WAI-ARIA APG Accordion Pattern\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**: In WAI-ARIA APG, each accordion header button must be enclosed in an element with `role=\"heading\"` having an appropriate `aria-level` (or native `<h3>`, `<h4>`, etc.). In `BuggyAccordion`, `<button className=\"accordion-trigger\">` is a direct child of `<div className=\"accordion-item\">`. Screen reader users rely on heading navigation (the `H` key or rotor/elements list) to skim documents and jump between sections. Without heading wrappers, screen reader users cannot locate or browse the accordion structure through heading navigation.\n- **Fix**: Wrap the trigger `<button>` inside an appropriate heading tag (e.g., `<h3>` or a configurable `headingLevel` prop).\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: a11y-accordion-missing-heading\nfingerprint: c4a8e2b910fd34a1\nsource: BuggyAccordion.jsx:13-22\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 1.3.1 Info and Relationships / WAI-ARIA APG Accordion Pattern\nsection_508_fpc_context: E205.4 / WCAG 2.0 1.3.1\nseverity: MAJOR\nperspective_alarms: screen_reader=HIGH, keyboard=LOW, low_vision=LOW, cognitive=MEDIUM\nevidence: <div className=\"accordion-item\"><button className=\"accordion-trigger\">...</button></div>\nreproduction_steps:\n  1. Inspect DOM structure of rendered accordion.\n  2. Navigate using screen reader heading shortcut ('H' in NVDA/JAWS or VO rotor).\n  3. Observe that accordion items are not discoverable as headings.\nexpected_behavior: Accordion button is contained within a semantic heading element (e.g. <h3><button ...></h3>).\nactual_behavior: Accordion button is a direct child of an unstyled <div> without heading semantics.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Expanded content panel lacks `role=\"region\"` and `aria-labelledby`\n- **Location**: `BuggyAccordion.jsx:24-29`\n- **Impacted Users**: Screen reader users\n- **Standard**: WCAG 2.2 SC 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships), SC 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value), WAI-ARIA APG Accordion Pattern\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**: Per the WAI-ARIA APG Accordion specification, accordion content panels should have `role=\"region\"` and be labeled by their controlling trigger via `aria-labelledby`. In `BuggyAccordion`, the panel is a generic `<div>` with only a class name and ID. When a screen reader user moves focus or virtual cursor into the panel, assistive technologies cannot announce the boundary of the region nor associate the content with the section title.\n- **Fix**: Assign `id={`trigger-${index}`}` to the `<button>`, and add `role=\"region\"` and `aria-labelledby={`trigger-${index}`}` to the panel `<div>`.\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: a11y-accordion-missing-panel-region\nfingerprint: d7e189a05cb49812\nsource: BuggyAccordion.jsx:24-29\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 1.3.1, WCAG 4.1.2 / WAI-ARIA APG Accordion Pattern\nsection_508_fpc_context: E205.4 / WCAG 2.0 1.3.1, 4.1.2\nseverity: MAJOR\nperspective_alarms: screen_reader=HIGH, keyboard=LOW, low_vision=LOW, cognitive=LOW\nevidence: <div id={`panel-${index}`} className=\"accordion-panel\">{item.content}</div>\nreproduction_steps:\n  1. Expand an accordion item.\n  2. Navigate virtual cursor into the expanded content panel.\n  3. Observe absence of landmark/region announcement or header label association.\nexpected_behavior: Panel has role=\"region\" and aria-labelledby pointing to the header button ID.\nactual_behavior: Panel is an unlabelled generic <div>.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n---\n\n### Minor Findings (Friction But Workaround Exists)\n\n#### 1. Dangling `aria-controls` reference when panels are collapsed\n- **Location**: `BuggyAccordion.jsx:18`, `BuggyAccordion.jsx:23`\n- **Impacted Users**: Screen reader users\n- **Standard**: WCAG 2.2 SC 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value)\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**: Line 18 unconditionally renders `aria-controls={`panel-${index}`}`, but line 23 conditionally unmounts the panel when `expanded !== index`:\n  ```jsx\n  {expanded === index && (\n    <div id={`panel-${index}`} className=\"accordion-panel\">...</div>\n  )}\n  ```\n  When collapsed, `aria-controls` references an ID that does not exist in the DOM. While modern screen readers degrade gracefully, referential integrity is broken.\n- **Fix**: Either keep panels in the DOM using `hidden={expanded !== index}` (or `style={{ display: expanded === index ? 'block' : 'none' }}`), or conditionally omit `aria-controls` when the element does not exist. Keeping the panel in the DOM with the `hidden` attribute preserves valid referential linkage.\n\n#### 2. Non-unique DOM IDs across multiple accordion instances\n- **Location**: `BuggyAccordion.jsx:18, 25`\n- **Impacted Users**: All assistive technology users, browser automation\n- **Standard**: WCAG 2.2 SC 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value), SC 1.3.1\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**: Hardcoding IDs as `panel-${index}` causes ID collisions if more than one `BuggyAccordion` is mounted on the page. Duplicate IDs break `aria-controls` and `aria-labelledby` resolution.\n- **Fix**: Generate an instance-specific prefix using React's `useId()` hook (e.g., `const baseId = useId();`, producing `id={`${baseId}-panel-${index}`}`).\n\n---\n\n### Enhancements (Best Practice Not Met)\n\n1. **APG Keyboard Navigation (Up/Down Arrow, Home/End)**:\n   - While native `<button>` provides standard Tab, Shift+Tab, Space, and Enter activation, WAI-ARIA APG recommends optional keyboard navigation among accordion headers:\n     - `ArrowDown`: Moves focus to next accordion trigger.\n     - `ArrowUp`: Moves focus to previous accordion trigger.\n     - `Home`: Moves focus to first accordion trigger.\n     - `End`: Moves focus to last accordion trigger.\n2. **Configurable Heading Level**:\n   - Provide a `headingLevel` prop (defaulting to `3`) so consumers can integrate the accordion into different depths of their page hierarchy (e.g., `h2`, `h3`, `h4`) without breaking heading order.\n\n---\n\n### What's Missing (Gaps & Unhandled Edge Cases)\n\n1. **Loss of Focus on Panel Collapse from Inside**:\n   - If `item.content` contains focusable elements (links, form fields, buttons) and the panel collapses (via external trigger, keyboard shortcut, or state update), unmounting the panel will dump focus to `<body>`. Focus management should restore focus to the header trigger if an unmounting panel contained document focus.\n2. **Missing Container Semantics or Grouping**:\n   - If the accordion represents an ordered FAQ or structured checklist, wrapping the accordion items in an accessible list structure (`<ul>` / `<li>`) provides list item counting context (\"1 of 5\") to screen readers.\n\n---\n\n### Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n- **Screen Reader User**: Severe navigation friction. Users cannot discover or skim accordion items using standard heading navigation shortcuts (`H` key). When navigating into the panel, they receive no announcement that they have entered a specific titled region.\n- **Keyboard-only User**: Basic functionality works (Tab reaches every button, Space/Enter toggles). However, arrow-key navigation is absent, requiring repeated Tab presses to traverse long accordions.\n- **Low Vision User**: Functionality is intact, but visual focus visibility (`:focus-visible` outline) and contrast of the `+`/`\u2212` toggle indicator depend entirely on external CSS stylesheets.\n- **Cognitive Accessibility**: Clear disclosure mechanism, but lack of programmatic heading structure makes document skimming harder for users relying on reader modes or assistive outlines.\n\n---\n\n### Perspective Alarm Levels\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | **HIGH** | Missing heading semantics, missing region role/label, broken `aria-controls` on collapsed panels |\n| Keyboard-only | **MEDIUM** | Tab works, but APG arrow keys absent and focus drop risk if internal element is active on collapse |\n| Low vision | **MEDIUM** | Focus indicator and text/icon contrast dependent on unverified CSS classes |\n| Cognitive | **MEDIUM** | Absence of heading structure impedes page readability tools |\n| Vestibular & motion | **LOW** | No animations or auto-motion present |\n| Auditory access | **LOW** | No audio or video elements |\n| Environmental contrast | **MEDIUM** | CSS styling for trigger and indicator must meet 3:1 (UI) and 4.5:1 (text) thresholds |\n\n---\n\n### Verdict Justification\n\n**VERDICT: REVISE**\n\nThe component cannot be marked `ACCEPT` or `ACCEPT-WITH-RESERVATIONS` because omitting heading semantics and region role associations directly violates core WAI-ARIA APG specifications and WCAG 1.3.1. These gaps degrade the primary navigation mode for screen reader users.\n\n**Requirements to upgrade to ACCEPT**:\n1. Enclose each accordion trigger button in a semantic heading tag (`<h[2-6]>`).\n2. Give each trigger button a unique `id` generated via React `useId()`.\n3. Add `role=\"region\"` and `aria-labelledby` referencing the trigger button on the content panel.\n4. Replace conditional unmounting with the HTML `hidden` attribute so that `aria-controls` targets remain valid DOM elements when collapsed.\n\n---\n\n### Remediated Implementation\n\n```jsx\nimport React, { useState, useId } from 'react';\n\nconst AccessibleAccordion = ({ items = [], headingLevel = 3 }) => {\n  const [expanded, setExpanded] = useState(0);\n  const baseId = useId();\n\n  const toggleItem = (index) => {\n    setExpanded(expanded === index ? -1 : index);\n  };\n\n  const HeadingTag = `h${headingLevel}`;\n\n  return (\n    <div className=\"accordion\">\n      {items.map((item, index) => {\n        const triggerId = `${baseId}-trigger-${index}`;\n        const panelId = `${baseId}-panel-${index}`;\n        const isExpanded = expanded === index;\n\n        return (\n          <div key={index} className=\"accordion-item\">\n            <HeadingTag className=\"accordion-heading\">\n              <button\n                id={triggerId}\n                className=\"accordion-trigger\"\n                type=\"button\"\n                onClick={() => toggleItem(index)}\n                aria-expanded={isExpanded}\n                aria-controls={panelId}\n              >\n                <span className=\"accordion-title\">{item.title}</span>\n                <span aria-hidden=\"true\" className=\"accordion-icon\">\n                  {isExpanded ? '\u2212' : '+'}\n                </span>\n              </button>\n            </HeadingTag>\n            <div\n              id={panelId}\n              role=\"region\"\n              aria-labelledby={triggerId}\n              className=\"accordion-panel\"\n              hidden={!isExpanded}\n            >\n              {item.content}\n            </div>\n          </div>\n        );\n      })}\n    </div>\n  );\n};\n\nexport default AccessibleAccordion;\n```\n\n---\n\n### Open Questions (Unscored)\n1. **Multi-panel expansion requirement**: Does the product requirement strictly require single-panel accordion behavior, or should users be allowed to expand multiple panels concurrently?\n2. **Page heading hierarchy context**: What is the typical heading hierarchy depth where this component is deployed? Making `headingLevel` configurable satisfies any parent context.\n",
